+{"type":"thought","data":" Let me break this down into actionable next steps based on what you've already surfaced."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nFor the SQLite side, the count mismatch injection looks promising—wrapping DELETE/ UPDATE statements to force incorrect change counts or skip deletes is a solid test fault pattern, especially since D."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"109b already had something similar."}
+{"type":"thought","data":" For memory, we'd need a similar hook into the TransitionOwner writes."}
+{"type":"thought","data":" IDB will probably need a custom transaction wrapper that tracks request counts or uses a fault-injected store."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nOn 12, getAll() returning everything is definitely feasible for a full scan, but we should profile it against the expected generation volume to ensure it doesn't blow up the transaction budget."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe transaction duration risk you flagged is real and worth a dedicated test case."}
+{"type":"thought","data":" The microtask behavior you described is exactly why the existing adapter pattern works 99% of the time, but we should add a test that forces a long sync computation right after the last getAll() completes to verify the transaction stays alive."}
+{"type":"thought","data":" That would catch any edge cases where the \"transaction active in microtasks\" fix doesn't hold."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nFor the worker termination vs IDB abort, testing both crash modes separately would be good—worker termination should trigger the abort path cleanly, while IDB quota exhaustion should also abort without partial writes."}
+{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nOn the encoding side, copyGenerationRecord for the none case and copyExpectedHead look solid."}
