+{"role":"tool","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system>708 lines read from file starting from line 1. Total lines in file: 88315. Max 102400 bytes reached.</system>"},{"type":"text","text":"     1\t# ts-drp-1 Production Hardening — Final Phased TDD Implementation Plan\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status:** build plan — supersedes `production-hardening-tdd-plan.md` (round 1)\n     4\t**Baseline:** `bf7d3516f6ed4be97a755698b4fb3a404e04dc0f` (`main`) — exactly the AHE v4 review baseline, zero drift\n     5\t**Goal:** take `ts-drp-1` from a research-grade signed operation-DAG to a production library capable of running an **MMORPG** or a **Discord-like chat at scale** — browser-first, hostile participants, churny NAT'd swarms.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Compaction decision (changed this round): implement Attested Hard Epochs v4 directly.** AHE v4 is the\n     8\tspecification, its reference implementation is the starting code, its wire formats are the wire formats, and\n     9\tits §23 acceptance checklist is our checklist. Where the spec and its reference disagree, or where either is\n    10\tincomplete, we **amend AHE v4 in-repo and regenerate the reference from the amendment** — we do not defer.\n    11\t\n    12\tThis plan synthesizes round 1, the six source documents, and a **twelve-agent adversarial review**\n    13\t(4× Opus-xhigh, 3× GPT-5.6-Sol-max, 2× Grok-4.5, 3× Kimi-K3), plus first-hand verification runs on this\n    14\tmachine. Where reviewers contradicted the source documents or each other, the code was re-read directly and\n    15\tthe correction is folded in with its evidence.\n    16\t\n    17\t---\n    18\t\n    19\t## 0. What changed, and what is now established fact\n    20\t\n    21\t### 0.1 Evidence produced on this machine (not inherited)\n    22\t\n    23\tRound 1 declined direct adoption largely because the reference was \"untyped JS outside the workspace with\n    24\tbrowser gates self-reported blocked.\" Both halves were tested here:\n    25\t\n    26\t| Check                     | Command                                                                                                                      | Result                                                                                                                                      |\n    27\t| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n    28\t| Reference test suite      | `node --test test/core.test.mjs` in `reference-implementation/`                                                              | **22/22 pass, 287 ms**                                                                                                                      |\n    29\t| Reference size            | `wc -l src/*.js`                                                                                                             | **2,379 LOC across 13 modules** (round 1's \"~3,400\" counted tests + browser harness)                                                        |\n    30\t| Browser gate, Chromium    | AHE `browser/harness.html` under the then-installed Playwright 1.51.1 (**historical measurement; not the current root pin**) | **PASS** — 3/3 checks, `elapsedMs 170.3`                                                                                                    |\n    31\t| Browser gate, Firefox 135 | same                                                                                                                         | **NO VERDICT — silent hang**                                                                                                                |\n    32\t| Browser gate, WebKit 605  | same                                                                                                                         | **NO VERDICT — silent hang**                                                                                                                |\n    33\t| Repo persistence infra    | `grep -rl \"indexedDB\" packages`                                                                                              | **zero matches**                                                                                                                            |\n    34\t| Repo Worker infra         | `grep -rl \"new Worker(\" packages`                                                                                            | **zero matches**                                                                                                                            |\n    35\t| CI shape                  | `.github/workflows/`                                                                                                         | 17 workflows, **all `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`**, most `timeout-minutes: 10`; `network-spike-public.yml` is 360 min (precedent for long jobs) |\n    36\t\n    37\tThe bundle's own `evidence/chromium-browser-validation.json` records `net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_ADMINISTRATOR`,\n    38\t`verdict: \"blocked\"`, with the note _\"the managed Chromium policy in this execution environment blocks all\n    39\tnavigation.\"_ That was environmental — but running it properly produces a **worse and more useful** result\n    40\tthan \"untested\":\n    41\t\n    42\t**Two of the three engines in AHE §21.5's own required release matrix produce no verdict at all — and the\n    43\tharness is structurally incapable of telling you so.**\n    44\t\n    45\tThe root cause is a **race in the harness, not a defect in the engines.** On both Firefox and WebKit\n    46\t`isSecureContext`, `crypto.subtle`, `indexedDB` and `navigator.locks` are all present, and `import()` of\n    47\t`canonical.js` (7 exports) and `indexeddb-store.js` (4 exports) both succeed on the main thread.\n    48\t`browser-tests.js:29-33` posts `{run:true}` **immediately** after `new Worker(\"./worker.js\",{type:\"module\"})`,\n    49\tbut the worker's module graph — five imports plus a **top-level `await` in `ct-merkle.js:3`** — has not yet\n    50\tinstalled `self.onmessage` (`worker.js:33`). Chromium happens to queue the message; Firefox and WebKit drop\n    51\tit, and the worker waits forever. **Delaying the post by 3 s makes both browsers pass the full compute\n    52\tpayload** (Firefox: encode 41 ms, merkle 87 ms; WebKit: encode 44 ms, merkle 80 ms). The standalone IDB\n    53\tvote-CAS also passes on both.\n    54\t\n    55\tThe damage is done by the missing timeout: `workerResponsiveness()` awaits a promise whose only settle paths\n    56\tare `onmessage` and `onerror`. With neither firing, `run()` never completes, `document.body` is never\n    57\twritten, and the page sits at `running…` forever. **The harness can report PASS; it cannot report FAIL for\n    58\tthis class.** A hang is indistinguishable from \"still running.\"\n    59\t\n    60\tTwo things follow. The directive's premise is confirmed — the matrix is executable here and Chromium is green\n    61\tend-to-end including the IDB stage/commit path. And the reference's browser story was **never actually\n    62\tvalidated cross-browser**: its static `browser_surface_audit.py` \"pass\" coexisted with a fatal cross-browser\n    63\truntime bug. Static audits are not browser gates.\n    64\t\n    65\tThis yields the rule that governs every gate below:\n    66\t\n    67\t> **An inherited gate is not a gate until it has been observed going red for the right reason, in this\n    68\t> repo's CI, on every engine in the matrix.**\n    69\t\n    70\t### 0.2 The inherited-evidence problem is larger than the browser gate\n    71\t\n    72\tThe AHE §22 headline numbers — 5,231 exhaustive DAGs, 10,000 randomized epochs, 20,000 delivery\n    73\tpermutations, 11,612 quorum-pair checks — come from **standalone Python scripts that never execute the JS\n    74\treference** (`model/validate_epoch_model.py`, `model/validate_seal.py`; stdlib-only imports, their own\n    75\t`kahn_order`, their own `fold_epoch`, their own digest). They validate a Python model against itself.\n    76\t`reference-implementation/package.json` runs `\"model\"` (Python) and `\"test\"` (JS) as separate scripts with\n    77\tno shared vectors — and **there are no golden vectors anywhere in the bundle**; all 22 JS tests compute\n    78\ttheir expectations at runtime.\n    79\t\n    80\tThe only executable evidence against the reference _code_ is those 22 tests, and\n    81\t`evidence/node-coverage.log` lists **11 of 13 modules** — `indexeddb-store.js` and `runtime.js` have **zero\n    82\texecuted coverage**. Those are precisely the crash-critical modules.\n    83\t\n    84\tConsequently: **no AHE number is a gate in this plan.** Every §21/§22 claim is re-derived by TS-driven\n    85\tenumerators that drive both engines over shared, checked-in vectors.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 0.3 Corrections to round 1 (verified against source at `bf7d351`)\n    88\t\n    89\tRound 1's evidence table is otherwise accurate — every `file:line` spot-checked by three independent\n    90\treviewers resolved correctly. These are the exceptions, and they change what gets built:\n    91\t\n    92\t1. **\"`FETCH_STATE` snapshots cross the wire unauthenticated\" is stale.** `fetchStateResponseHandler`\n    93\t   (`packages/node/src/handlers.ts:196-209`) **never adopts** network state — the code says so explicitly:\n    94\t   _\"Network-provided ACL/DRP state snapshots are never adopted… Either way the state is dropped.\"_\n    95\t   `DRPObject.setACLState` and `merge(_, rootACLState)` both throw on root-ACL adoption. Genesis authority\n    96\t   is derived locally from the creator-bound object id, so there is **no authority-takeover to fix**.\n    97\t   What remains is the _responder_ (`handlers.ts:153-168`), which still calls `getStates()`, serializes the\n    98\t   full snapshot and ships it to a peer that discards it — a free **amplification-DoS**, cheap to request,\n    99\t   `O(state-bytes)` of CPU and egress on the victim. Different fix, different phase, much smaller.\n   100\t2. **Hash-framing endianness is not in conflict.** Spec §4.2 and `hash.js` are _both_ big-endian for the\n   101\t   framing integers. The real divergence is the **canonical typed-array profile**: spec §4.1 mandates\n   102\t   little-endian, `canonical.js:76-79,117-136` encodes big-endian.\n   103\t3. **`blueprintDigest` is already in the reference's cut descriptor** (`protocol.js:142,165`). It is missing\n   104\t   from the reference's **anchor** (`protocol.js:176-201`) and **snapshot payload** (`snapshot.js:102-104`).\n   105\t   Round 1 listed it as simply \"omitted.\"\n   106\t4. **The invalid-hash FIFO is memory-bounded.** `MAX_KNOWN_INVALID_VERTEX_HASHES = 10_000` with\n   107\t   evict-oldest (`drp-applier.ts:34,282-289`). Rotation does not exhaust memory; it flips evicted invalid\n   108\t   _parents_ back to `missing`, driving `recoverMissingSync` re-request churn. The gate must assert\n   109\t   **re-request rate**, not megabytes — as written it passes trivially.\n   110\t5. **Wall-clock invalidity is not \"permanent poisoning.\"** A rejected vertex is not in the graph, so\n   111\t   redelivery re-runs validation with a fresh clock. The repairable case is narrower than the original\n   112\t   wording implied: only a finite submitted timestamp strictly beyond a finite receiver clock is\n   113\t   receiver-clock-dependent and therefore re-requestable. Dependency-relative temporal violations,\n   114\t   submitted infinities and downstream throws of the public timestamp-error class are deterministic\n   115\t   terminal failures. Phase 0f makes the narrow receiver-future case `pending` instead of dropping it as\n   116\t   `invalid`; otherwise two honest replicas at different clock offsets can converge to different graphs\n   117\t   and never repair. Live silent divergence, independent of compaction.\n   118\t6. **`perf-contracts.test.ts` was green when this audit was written, but is no longer green.** Its authenticated\n   119\t   pre-regression figures were 8/8 at ~167 ms total, with the MapDRP case at 52.8 ms against a 1,000 ms\n   120\t   limit; a later 197.4 ms run was also green. Phase 1d(i) then introduced the comparison-cost regression\n   121\t   owned by D.92.3-P1 below. Current exact-parent/current medians are ~6.87/~6.64 seconds at the unchanged\n   122\t   threshold. The `describe(... RED until optimized)` label is therefore factually RED again, but for the\n   123\t   recorded regression rather than the original optimization backlog. The Phase 1a sync-compare test remains\n   124\t   net-new rather than an extension of this MapDRP case.\n   125\t7. **`benchmark.yml:88` and `benchmark-memory.yml:64` both set `fail-on-alert: false`**, and the PR\n   126\t   benchmark job is `continue-on-error`. Round 1 wires the Phase-6 memory-slope gate — the single most\n   127\t   important economic proof in the program — into workflows engineered never to block anything.\n   128\t8. **The global coverage gate cannot catch the failure it exists to catch.** `scripts/coverage.ts:4`\n   129\t   enforces one summed 70% line threshold; a module at zero coverage passes if everything else covers.\n   130\t   That is exactly how the bundle shipped \"91.71%\" while excluding its two crash-critical modules.\n   131\t9. **`runtime.js` is imported by nothing** — `browser/worker.js:1-5` imports five _other_ modules. The\n   132\t   bundle's \"Worker responsiveness\" evidence executes different code than the module it appears to validate.\n   133\t10. Round 1's own doc-corrections stand and are confirmed: the \"insertion-sensitive DFS\" claim is stale\n   134\t    (`hashgraph/index.ts:318` `.sort()`s forward edges — target **origin**-sensitivity); the AEC contraction\n   135\t    counterexample uses non-antichain deps; `@chainsafe/bls ^8.1.0` is already a production dependency of\n   136\t    `object` and `keychain` (via `@chainsafe/bls/herumi`, the WASM backend — so AHE §2.2's\n   137\t    \"avoid a BLS/WASM dependency\" rationale is _doubly_ stale).\n   138\t\n   139\t### 0.4 The verdict, restated\n   140\t\n   141\tImplementing AHE v4 directly is the right call, and the reference is a genuine asset — but \"directly\" means\n   142\t**freeze first, then port**, never \"transliterate the reference.\" Three of its modules are safe to port\n   143\tnearly as-is; two must be _rewritten_ rather than ported; and the protocol has four genuine gaps that must\n   144\tbe closed as normative amendments before any consensus byte is written:\n   145\t\n   146\t| #   | Gap                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Resolution (Phase −1)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n   147\t| --- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   148\t| G1  | No single frozen encoding. Spec recommends CBOR + LE typed arrays; the reference is a proprietary tag codec + BE. Several consensus objects have **no normative preimage at all**.                               | One frozen registry (§4). Reference regenerated from it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n   149\t| G2  | `round` lives inside the signed cut descriptor, so a locked value cannot be re-proposed. **This is a real, constructible n=4 permanent stall.**                                                                  | Split round-free `CutValue` from round-bearing `SealProposal`; QCs bind `valueDigest`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n   150\t| G3  | The pacemaker is six bullets; the reference **rejects the `round-change` phase its own vote schema defines** (`seal.js:21-35`) and omits `highestPrepareQC`.                                                     | Normative pacemaker (§Phase 5), model-checked before attested mode.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n   151\t| G4  | Admission fails open — ancestry is optional (`admission.js:74-83`), and no signature, ACL, schema or invariant check runs before `accept` (`admission.js:41-91`; `fold.js:38` defaults authorization to `true`). | Phase 0d owns the mandatory fail-closed admission pipeline, Phase 0e owns the exact package-level ancestor index and strict boolean result contract, Phase 3a owns the first live v3 binder, and Phase 4a owns removal of the fold's default-`true` authorization. The accepted atomic 0d/0e v2 library boundary is a preservation baseline, not production integration: G4 remains open until the Phase-3a v3 binder lands on Phase 0q's commit discipline and Phase 4a removes the legacy fail-open fold. |\n   152\t\n   153\tAnd two structural facts constrain the whole program:\n   154\t\n   155\t- **Compaction is a divergence amplifier.** The instant any peer adopts a snapshot instead of replaying,\n   156\t  _any_ nondeterminism becomes a permanent silent fork. The determinism foundation is a hard prerequisite,\n   157\t  no exceptions.\n   158\t- **AHE bounds the history age of _one object_. It is not a multiplayer fabric.** Shipping AHE and calling\n   159\t  the stack MMORPG-ready is marketing. The ephemeral, sharding and topology planes (Tracks E/S/T) are\n   160\t  load-bearing for the stated goal and sit entirely outside AHE.\n   161\t\n   162\t### 0.5 Two release trains — do not merge the claims\n   163\t\n   164\t| Train                                  | Contents                                                                                                                                  | Claim it earns                                                                                                |\n   165\t| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   166\t| **Train C — Correctness & Compaction** | Phases −1…7. Single-object AHE v4: determinism, durability, v2 envelope, seal, bounded pruning, archive.                                  | _\"Production-hardened signed-command rooms with bounded history.\"_ Serverless is honest here for small rooms. |\n   167\t| **Train S — Fabric**                   | Tracks E, S, T + the Profile gates. Ephemeral simulation plane, sharding/cross-object conservation, mesh topology and connection budgets. | _\"MMORPG or Discord at scale.\"_ **Only when Train S gates are green.** Requires operator-run relays.          |\n   168\t\n   169\t**Train C preserves the zero-deploy profile.** Today the grid and chat examples run end-to-end over\n   170\t_public_ infrastructure — Nostr relays for discovery, public delegated routing for relay candidates, no\n   171\tself-deployed fixtures (`examples/grid/playwright.fully-public.config.ts`, `pnpm e2e-test:fully-public`).\n   172\tNothing in Train C changes that: creator-trusted sealing needs one signer (the creator's client),\n   173\t`local-only` pruning needs no mirror, and durable storage is the peer's own. This is a **stated invariant\n   174\twith a regression gate**: the fully-public e2e stays in the weekly tier (reports-only — it depends on live\n   175\tthird-party infra and is flaky by its own comments), and a pre-release run at the release SHA must be\n   176\ttriaged (regression vs infra outage) before Train C ships. The operator node buys things that are\n   177\t_currently impossible_ (attested quorum liveness, relay-spine fanout, rooms that outlive their members) —\n   178\tit must never become a requirement for things that already work with none.\n   179\t\n   180\tTrain S is not a backlog. Several of its slices (connection budgets, the O(1) applied-set index, the crypto\n   181\tWorker queue, permissioned defaults) are **prerequisites of Train C's own scale gates** and land in Phase 1.\n   182\t\n   183\t### 0.6 The honest scope statement\n   184\t\n   185\tThree things are out of reach for a pure browser mesh regardless of plan quality, and all three resolve with\n   186\tthe same component — an **operator-run node**, untrusted for state (bytes verified, cuts QC-checked, referee\n   187\tops signed and disputable):\n   188\t\n   189\t1. **Ephemeral fanout above ~16 players/zone.** A browser cannot originate 30 Hz × 63-peer upload fanout.\n   190\t   Interest management reduces _relevance_, not the sender's fanout. An SFU-style relay is required.\n   191\t2. **Compaction liveness under tab churn.** AHE §2.3's fallback — _\"the active epoch simply grows until a\n   192\t   configured hard safety limit\"_ — reintroduces unbounded history exactly when quorum churns. At the\n   193\t   default `maxEpochVertices = 8192` and a modest 5 durable ops/s that is ~27 minutes to the hard limit.\n   194\t   Browser tabs also have a _negative_ incentive to sign (burn battery for closes benefiting latecomers)\n   195\t   and get OS-suspended.\n   196\t3. **Contested-outcome validity.** Attributability without a referee is not anti-cheat.\n   197\t\n   198\tAdd to this the finding that **no local-only algorithm can distinguish first signer installation from\n   199\tcomplete origin-storage eviction** (§Phase 5) — so browser tabs cannot be **seal voters** by default.\n   200\tSigning is unaffected (every vertex, ACL op and trade is signed in-browser today); the constraint is the\n   201\tnever-vote-twice slot obligation, and two routes let a browser hold it anyway: at `q = 1`, a recoverable\n   202\tseed-derived key plus a mandatory re-learn-from-peers rule (§Phase 5, slice 5e — safe because every vote at\n   203\t`q = 1` _is_ a QC and lives on in the network); at quorum `≥ 2`, every voter preserves exact-slot\n   204\tanti-equivocation continuity through either detectable-loss durable storage, an external exact-slot\n   205\twitness, or — for a browser-local eviction-prone voter without such a witness — a **fate-shared\n   206\tnon-extractable key** co-evicted with the vote log (§Phase 5, custody options 1–3). Trust profile determines\n   207\tauthority and quorum; storage class determines custody. Both delegated and attested sets admit durable and\n   208\tbrowser-local voters. The liveness cost of fate-sharing is why an always-on durable-class signer remains\n   209\tin the picture as a **liveness backstop, not a trust requirement**.\n   210\t\n   211\t**Optional desktop clients are the P2P route to a durable tier.** If the product ships an **Electron\n   212\tbuild alongside the web app** — optional, opt-in, never required — any participant running it is\n   213\tdurable-class by construction: no ITP, no 7-day clock, storage in the app's own data directory rather than\n   214\ta browser profile competing with a thousand origins. One such participant collapses **two of the three\n   215\toperator roles onto a peer**: signer (durable vote log) and mirror (it has a disk). Only **relay** does not\n   216\tfollow, since a home machine behind NAT is usually not reachable. Two consequences: the fate-shared-key\n   217\tproblem largely dissolves (key and vote log share an app-data directory, or the OS keychain), and\n   218\ttray-resident / launch-on-login — normal for a desktop app, impossible for a tab — is what converts\n   219\t_durable_ into _durable and usually online_. Treat that as a deliberate product decision, not a side\n   220\teffect. It does **not** auto-enroll anyone: the signer set is still pinned in the anchor and changed only\n   221\tby handoff (§Phase 5, 5e2), so a desktop user is a _good_ delegate, not automatically _a_ delegate.\n   222\t\n   223\t**What the operator node concretely is:** not a new service — the same `DRPNode`, long-lived, in Node.js\n   224\t(`pnpm cli` → `packages/node/src/run.ts`). The relay role already exists as config\n   225\t(`configs/bootstrap.json`: `\"relay_service\": { \"enabled\": true }`); mirror and signer land as two more\n   226\trole flags on that same process. Train C requires none of them beyond the signaling broker every\n   227\tbrowser-to-browser system needs — which may be public infrastructure (§0.5, zero-deploy) or self-run.\n   228\t\n   229\t**P2P-first is a design rule, not a preference.** \"Durable-class\" (§Phase 5) is a storage property, not an\n   230\townership property — a friend's always-on desktop running `pnpm cli` qualifies exactly as a company-run\n   231\tmirror does. Every trust profile has a working zero-infrastructure configuration (§1, principle 11); an\n   232\toperator-run mirror/relay MAY additionally enroll as an attestor later, but operator participation is\n   233\topt-in and additive — never load-bearing for correctness, and never a precondition for any profile to\n   234\texist.\n   235\t\n   236\t**Therefore:** reachable target is zone-instanced multiplayer (≤ 64 durable writers/zone, 20–30 Hz\n   237\tephemeral, operator relay spine) and Discord-scale guilds (per-channel objects, ≤ 1k–5k online/channel,\n   238\tarchive profile). **Seamless single-shard worlds and serverless competitive anti-cheat are non-goals.**\n   239\tProduct decision already recorded: build without mirrors now, design assuming operator-owned mirrors later.\n   240\t\n   241\t---\n   242\t\n   243\t## 1. Principles\n   244\t\n   245\t1. **Freeze before you port.** No consensus byte is written before the Phase −1 registry is merged. One\n   246\t   immutable golden-vector document is minted per registry version. Before reference regeneration, the only\n   247\t   lawful extension is an exact `registryVersion + 1` transition with exactly one new vector document that\n   248\t   carries every prior vector record byte-identically. Regeneration closes that window; any later consensus\n   249\t   change requires `protocolMajor: 3`. A vector diff must be impossible to land silently.\n   250\t2. **Compaction amplifies divergence.** The determinism foundation gates every snapshot-trusting feature.\n   251\t3. **Classify every change.** _replica-local-safe_ (ship now, legacy plane) / _coordinated-upgrade_\n   252\t   (observable post-merge state or wire format; test cross-version explicitly) / _consensus-affecting_\n   253\t   (**exactly one frozen protocol-major namespace, never patched in place, never dual-preimage under one\n   254\t   `objectId`; v2 remains immutable and v3 is its successor**).\n   255\t   A misclassification is a silent permanent fork — and round 1 misclassified three of its own slices (§Phase 0).\n   256\t4. **Every gate names an executable evidence artifact** — a test file, a model output, a crash-injection\n   257\t   log, a browser-matrix JSON, a golden-vector file. No inherited number is a gate.\n   258\t5. **Every gate must be provably able to fail — and a RED spec is evidence only as a pair _(baseline, current)_.** Before its GREEN half begins, every new RED spec file is executed against the baseline SHA in a throwaway git worktree (**never** `git stash` — reviewers share one tree), and the pair is recorded with the text of the failing assertion; a bare count is satisfiable by a broken harness. `FAIL/FAIL` is a genuine RED contract; `PASS/FAIL` is a **regression pin**; `PASS/PASS` is **not a gate** and must be strengthened or deleted; `FAIL/PASS` is already green. _Scope qualifier:_ the baseline leg binds only where the subject code exists at baseline — for `protocol-v2`, `seal`, `storage-browser` and the other new packages a baseline run fails on module resolution, and **an import error is not a RED**; there, run the spec before the implementation exists and record that it fails on the asserted contract. A regression pin must assert the **invariant**, never the baseline's behaviour: pinning the old semantics as a \"positive control\" makes the pin unsatisfiable alongside the slice that changes them (D.5(i)). Each differential/oracle gate additionally ships a **mutation probe**:\n   259\t   a seeded defect the gate must catch. The repo already has this pattern\n   260\t   (`packages/object/tests/proptest/mutation-check.test.ts`); it is the only known defense against an oracle\n   261\t   drifting green.\n   262\t6. **Every zero/never assertion ships a positive control** in the same test. _\"Zero durable vertices\"_\n   263\t   passes trivially if nothing is wired; assert in the same run that a durable command **does** create\n   264\t   exactly one vertex.\n   265\t7. **Atomic vs sliceable is explicit and argued — and every \"atomic\" names its observable boundary.**\n   266\t   For the legacy applier that boundary is _one vertex_ across **all** of: hashgraph (vertices, forward and\n   267\t   backward edges, frontier membership **and frontier order**, which feeds default dependency order and\n   268\t   therefore signed bytes), per-hash state snapshots, the finality store, the live `drp`/`acl` proxies,\n   269\t   `checkpoints`, `knownInvalidVertexHashes`, and subscriber notification. Two invariants come with it: the\n   270\t   owner stores are never rebound (identity is stable), and nothing is published before it is committed.\n   271\t   Atomic: the registry freeze itself; vertex preimage;\n   272\t   canonical-order switch; staged state-adoption semantics; **the vote slot + signer state + outbox as one\n   273\t   transaction** (a half-durable vote slot is _worse_ than none — it enables double-signing); the adoption\n   274\t   pointer swap; latched-ACL genesis semantics.\n   275\t8. **Shadow before destructive**, and **observation before acting**. Snapshots are produced, verified and\n   276\t   digest-compared across independent replicas plus archival replay before anything is pruned; seal computes\n   277\t   and compares cuts before it certifies them.\n   278\t9. **Extend the existing test muscle.** `packages/test-utils/src/property-harness.ts` (seeded mulberry32,\n   279\t   fake-timer virtual clock), the `linearize-reference.test.ts` independent-oracle pattern, the proptest\n   280\t   convergence/partition suites, `perf-contracts.test.ts`'s instrumented-probe-counter pattern, the\n   281\t   `failure-campaign` harness. A gate that does not run in CI will rot.\n   282\t10. **Scale is stated as per-object ceilings, in a profile table, bound to a named harness.** Anything not\n   283\t    in a profile table is not a supported scale claim.\n   284\t11. **P2P-first: every trust profile has a working zero-infrastructure configuration.** Operator-run nodes\n   285\t    (relay / mirror / signer, and optionally attestor later) are opt-in and additive — one way to obtain\n   286\t    durability or fanout, never the definition of either and never a requirement. A slice that makes any\n   287\t    profile depend on operator infrastructure to _exist_ is a regression against this principle (the\n   288\t    zero-deploy invariant, §0.5, is the Train-C instance of the same rule).\n   289\t\n   290\t---\n   291\t\n   292\t## 2. Package topology and the AHE port map\n   293\t\n   294\t### 2.1 Packages\n   295\t\n   296\tRespecting the verified dependency direction (`object` depends only on `logger`/`tracer`/`types`/`utils`/\n   297\t`validation`; `node` is the composition root; `object` never depends on `network`):\n   298\t\n   299\t```text\n   300\tpackages/protocol-v2/     frozen codec, domain-separated hashing, field registry, v2 envelopes,\n   301\t                          admission, signature suite            (no deps beyond types/utils)\n   302\tpackages/compaction/      Kahn order, fold, snapshots, close manifest, RFC 9162 history +\n   303\t                          archive-index roots                   (below node, no network dep)\n   304\tpackages/storage/         runtime-neutral durable-store contract, state machine, returned rejection taxonomy,\n   305\t                          shared fault scenarios, in-memory model (durability: \"ephemeral\"); depends only on\n   306\t                          @ts-drp/canonical and never imports a protocol package\n   307\tpackages/storage-browser/ IndexedDB backend: schema, migrations, immutable exact-byte CAS,\n   308\t                          staged generations, vote slots, kill-point injection subpath\n   309\tpackages/storage-node/    SQLite backend: composite keys, WAL, full synchronous durability,\n   310\t                          also the backend for optional Electron clients (main process) — never storage-browser,\n   311\t                          SIGKILL crash tests\n   312\tpackages/seal/            round-free CutValue, proposals, votes, QCs, locks, pacemaker,\n   313\t                          authority handoff                     (separate from FinalityStore)\n   314\tpackages/sync-v2/         object-head exchange, resumable content-addressed chunk fetch,\n   315\t                          bounded active-tail reconciliation\n   316\tpackages/worker-host/     bounded streaming execution, closed telemetry, ready handshake;\n   317\t                          no Worker construction, hashing, reducer, or fold ownership\n   318\tpackages/ephemeral/       Track E: non-durable simulation plane (cannot create vertices, by construction)\n   319\t```\n   320\t\n   321\t`packages/storage/` is not optional bookkeeping. Without a runtime-neutral contract, IDB and Node semantics\n   322\tdiverge and green Node CI becomes poor evidence for browser correctness. The **same** exported\n   323\t`runStoreContract(factory)` runs in Vitest against Node/SQLite and in Playwright against real IndexedDB.\n   324\t\n   325\tThe legacy plane (`object` applier/pipeline, legacy hash/topic) keeps running unchanged; v2 rooms live on a\n   326\tseparate namespace and topic. This converts a risky migration into an **additive parallel build** with one\n   327\tsigned migration step at the end.\n   328\t\n   329\t### 2.2 Port map — what happens to each of the 13 reference modules\n   330\t\n   331\t| Reference module     | LOC  | Disposition                                                                                                                                     | Target            |\n   332\t| -------------------- | ---- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n   333\t| `canonical.js`       | 390  | **Port + amend** (typed-array `-0`, endianness decision, locale-free sorting)                                                                   | `protocol-v2`     |\n   334\t| `hash.js`            | 82   | **Port as-is** — its framing becomes normative                                                                                                  | `protocol-v2`     |\n   335\t| `protocol.js`        | ~200 | **Port + amend** (add omitted preimage fields; delete `round` from the cut; add trust profile)                                                  | `protocol-v2`     |\n   336\t| `admission.js`       | ~120 | **Port + amend** — the fail-open pipeline is replaced wholesale (G4)                                                                            | `protocol-v2`     |\n   337\t| `linearize.js`       | 230  | **Port + harden/amend** (min-hash order; deterministic validation; five-action, isolated, fail-closed resolver contract; bounded swap cycles)   | `compaction`      |\n   338\t| `fold.js`            | 74   | **Port + amend** (`fold.js:38` defaults authorization to `true` — must fail closed)                                                             | `compaction`      |\n   339\t| `state.js`           | 90   | **Partial port + amend** (`stateDigest` only, synchronous canonical hashing; replacement is 0c and the reference DSM is not ported)             | `compaction`      |\n   340\t| `snapshot.js`        | 106  | **Port + amend** (payload omits `blueprintDigest`, `archiveIndexRoot`)                                                                          | `compaction`      |\n   341\t| `archive.js`         | 196  | **Port as-is**                                                                                                                                  | `compaction`      |\n   342\t| `ct-merkle.js`       | 201  | **Port + harden/amend** (synchronous RFC 9162 primitives; copy-safe inputs/snapshots, proof validation, batching and safe accumulator counting) | `compaction`      |\n   343\t| `seal.js`            | ~280 | **Port + major amend** (round-free CutValue, `round-change` phase, `highestPrepareQC`, durable state)                                           | `seal`            |\n   344\t| `indexeddb-store.js` | 255  | **REWRITE — do not port.** Zero coverage; audit below. Kept as a _negative_ regression source.                                                  | `storage-browser` |\n   345\t| `runtime.js`         | ~90  | **REPLACE — do not port.** Unbounded, imported by nothing, not a Worker host.                                                                   | `worker-host`     |\n   346\t\n   347\t**Why the last two are rewrites, not ports.** `indexeddb-store.js` has similarly-named methods but does not\n   348\timplement AHE §14.3/§16 semantics. Verified defects: a rejected `\"strict\"` transaction is **silently retried\n   349\twith default durability** (`:27-33`); chunk and manifest writes use `put`, so different bytes can overwrite\n   350\tan allegedly immutable digest (`:102-121`); `commitEpoch` receives **no QC and performs no authority\n   351\tverification** (`:123-128`) and does **no current-anchor CAS**, so two adopters are last-writer-wins\n   352\t(`:160-175`); it performs **unbounded destructive deletion of the entire old tail inside the pointer\n   353\ttransaction** (`:148-159`), making cleanup part of commit — directly contrary to AHE §16.2; recovery reads\n   354\thead and journal in **two concurrent transactions** and can observe a combination that never existed\n   355\tatomically (`:206-213`); `getVotes` is an unbounded `getAll()` scan across every room (`:91-96`); the vote\n   356\tslot stores a structured-cloned object rather than exact signed bytes (`:73-89`). Transliterating this\n   357\twould make a typed port _look_ durable while permitting lost votes, conflicting anchors, immutable-content\n   358\tcorruption and rollback deletion.\n   359\t\n   360\t`runtime.js`: `batchSize` unvalidated (0 loops forever), cancellation checked once per batch, every output\n   361\tretained (`O(total output)` memory rather than streaming), unbounded counter cardinality, every timing\n   362\tsample retained forever, and `Math.max(0, ...values)` eventually exceeds engine argument limits.\n   363\t\n   364\t### 2.3 The oracle architecture\n   365\t\n   366\tFour oracles, each proving something different. The critical subtlety: **a reference regenerated from our own\n   367\tamended schema is a second transcription of our own decisions** — conformance against it proves transcription\n   368\tfidelity, not spec fidelity. So both versions are kept, with different jobs.\n   369\t\n   370\t| Oracle                               | Proves                                                                                               | Cannot prove                                                                             | Kept honest by                                                                                                                               |\n   371\t| ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   372\t| **(a) Legacy engine differential**   | v2 diverges from legacy _exactly_ where the manifest says; zero accidental drift on the legacy plane | That either engine is correct (legacy is the buggy one)                                  | Approved-divergence manifest with **exercised-entry enforcement** + mutation probe; legacy pinned at `bf7d351`                               |\n   373\t| **(b) Pinned original JS reference** | Wire/digest conformance with an _independent_ reading of AHE v4, on the **un-amended** subset        | Anything about amended areas; storage behavior (zero coverage)                           | Commit-pinned and **edit-forbidden**. Amended areas covered by hand-reviewed vectors instead                                                 |\n   374\t| **(c) Regenerated JS reference**     | The amended schema is implementable twice and the vectors are reproducible                           | Spec fidelity (it is our own decisions, restated)                                        | **Different author** from the TS port; both pinned to the registry                                                                           |\n   375\t| **(d) Archival replay**              | Snapshot induction `S_(e+1) = Fold(S_e, KahnOrder(C_e))` ≡ from-scratch replay                       | Availability; blueprint semantic correctness (a buggy reducer replays identically wrong) | Must go through the public vertex-ingest path and share **no code** with the fold/adoption pipeline except the frozen codec and the reducers |\n   376\t\n   377\tPlus **(e) an independent linearization re-implementation** in the existing `linearize-reference.test.ts`\n   378\tstyle, written from the spec text by a different author, importing nothing from `packages/compaction`.\n   379\t\n   380\t**Where the references live.** The byte-identical original is\n   381\t`packages/protocol-v2/conformance/ahe-reference/`; it was moved from the review bundle without editing its\n   382\tthirteen source files. The independently regenerated implementation lands separately at\n   383\t`packages/protocol-v2/conformance/ahe-reference-regen/`. Both stay untyped ESM, excluded from\n   384\t`tsconfig.build.json` and the package `files` array; root `tsconfig.json` already sets `allowJs: true` and\n   385\tNode ≥20 provides `globalThis.crypto.subtle`, so vitest imports them directly. The original and regenerated\n   386\ttrees are never overlaid: they are different oracles with different provenance.\n   387\t\n   388\t**The anti-\"fixing\" mechanism** (without this the oracle is worthless): commit\n   389\t`packages/protocol-v2/conformance/reference.lock.json` — SHA-256 of every file under `ahe-reference/src/`,\n   390\tbijectively checked against the review bundle's `SHA256SUMS.txt`. The original tree and lock are immutable:\n   391\t**no registry bump authorizes changing either one**. A pure-Node, every-PR checker is executed from the\n   392\tmerge-base after bootstrap, so a PR cannot grade changes with its own replacement checker. The one head-checker\n   393\trun is permitted only on a stacked base that already contains registry v5 and the original reference but no\n   394\tfreeze policy. CODEOWNERS routes the original and regenerated trees, both locks, the registry, vectors,\n   395\tpolicy, checker and workflow to the same protocol-owner cohort. The base checker validates the effective\n   396\tprotocol CODEOWNERS tail block and workflow security shape semantically, allowing unrelated ownership and\n   397\taction-version maintenance without weakening the gate. Golden-vector documents are immutable; while Phase −1 remains open, an exact\n   398\t`+1` registry transition may add one byte-identical superset document. Slice −1f then adds\n   399\t`reference-regen.lock.json`, whose provenance pins registry v5, the v5 vectors and the original-reference\n   400\tlock; once that pair exists, the registry and both oracle ratchets freeze permanently.\n   401\t\n   402\t### 2.4 Three live defects in the reference, confirmed by execution here\n   403\t\n   404\tThese would be inherited by a faithful port. All three were found by review and re-verified by me directly.\n   405\t\n   406\t**D1 — `localeCompare` in three consensus-critical sorts is a fork vector.**\n   407\t`protocol.js:63` sorts the signer set (feeding `signerSetDigest`, which is in the anchor preimage at\n   408\t`protocol.js:199`); `seal.js:77` sorts QC votes before the QC digest; `archive.js:163` sorts archive-index\n   409\tentries before the Merkle root. `localeCompare` with no locale argument uses the host's ICU data. Measured:\n   410\t\n   411\t```\n   412\tids            = ['peer-B','peer-a','Peer-a','peer_a','peerä']\n   413\tlocaleCompare  → peer_a, peer-a, Peer-a, peer-B, peerä\n   414\tcodepoint      → Peer-a, peer-B, peer-a, peer_a, peerä\n   415\t```\n   416\t\n   417\tTwo replicas with different default locales compute different `signerSetDigest` → different anchor hash →\n   418\t**permanent fork**, or two disjoint quorums certifying different anchors for the same cut.\n   419\t_Resolution:_ UTF-8 byte order equals codepoint order, not UTF-16 code-unit order. All protocol sorts use\n   420\tthat order, fixed per field in the registry as `sortRule: \"codepoint\"`. The registry also constrains the `signerId` charset (no control\n   421\tcharacters), which additionally closes the `\\0` key-smuggling in the NUL-delimited vote key\n   422\t(`seal.js:264`, `indexeddb-store.js:37`) — replaced by native IDB array keys.\n   423\t\n   424\t**D2 — `Float32Array` `-0` encodes to bytes the reference's own decoder rejects.** Measured:\n   425\t\n   426\t```\n   427\tencodeCanonical(Float32Array.of(-0))  → 0b0180000000        (succeeds)\n   428\tdecodeCanonical(0b0180000000)         → throws \"typed array contains non-canonical negative zero\"\n   429\tencode(f32 -0) === encode(f32 +0)     → false\n   430\t```\n   431\t\n   432\tThe encoder normalizes `-0` for `number` (`canonical.js:104`) and `Float64Array` (`:127`) but **not**\n   433\t`Float32Array` (`:121`); the decoder rejects `-0` in any typed array (`:357`). `Float32Array` is the tag\n   434\taimed squarely at game state. A physics field that ever produces `-0` (trivially: `0 * -1`) exports a\n   435\tsnapshot **no conforming peer can decode** — a room-wide unrecoverable close failure.\n   436\t_Resolution:_ registry amendment — the encoder MUST normalize `-0 → 0` in `Float32Array`.\n   437\t\n   438\t**D3 — admission verifies the digest before the cheap identity checks.** `admission.js:44-50` canonically\n   439\tencodes and SHA-256s the vertex _before_ comparing `objectId`/`protocolMajor`/`epoch`, so a flood of\n   440\twrong-room garbage costs full crypto per message — violating spec §18.3's \"reject oversize input before deep\n   441\tdecode.\" _Resolution:_ reorder to syntactic/limit checks → identity equality → digest → dependencies.\n   442\t\n   443\t### 2.5 The typing rules for a semantics-preserving JS→TS port\n   444\t\n   445\tAdding types changes behaviour in specific, enumerable places. Each rule below is derived from actual\n   446\treference code and carries the test that catches a violation. This table lands as\n   447\t`packages/protocol-v2/docs/porting-rules.md`, cross-referenced from the registry.\n   448\t\n   449\t| #   | Hazard                               | Reference evidence                                                                                                                                           | Port rule                                                                                                                                                                                        | Violation-catching test                                                                                                                                 |\n   450\t| --- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   451\t| 1   | number vs bigint                     | integers are zigzag-varuint of **safe `number`s**; `bigint` throws (`canonical.js:106,111-113`); decode rejects beyond ±(2⁵³−1) (`:271`)                     | protocol integers are `number` + runtime `Number.isSafeInteger`; never widen to `bigint`                                                                                                         | vector: `2^53−1` round-trips; `encode(2^53)` and `encode(1n)` throw                                                                                     |\n   452\t| 2   | `-0` / NaN                           | D2 above                                                                                                                                                     | all float paths normalize `-0`, reject non-finite                                                                                                                                                | D2 round-trip property                                                                                                                                  |\n   453\t| 3   | `undefined` vs absent key            | `{a: undefined}` **throws** (`canonical.js:111-112`); absence is the only omission                                                                           | new packages compile with `exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true`; builders omit, never assign `undefined`                                                                                            | `@ts-expect-error` on `{field: undefined}`; builder output for an absent optional field digests equal to the reference                                  |\n   454\t| 4   | prototypes / `Object.create(null)`   | decoder returns null-proto objects (`canonical.js:309`); encoder accepts only null-proto and `Object.prototype` (`:71-74`), rejects class instances (`:178`) | decoded values typed `Record<string, CanonicalValue>`; use `Object.hasOwn`, never `.hasOwnProperty`                                                                                              | assert `Object.getPrototypeOf(decoded) === null`; a ts-proto message instance and a class instance both throw                                           |\n   455\t| 5   | Map/Set iteration order              | encoder sorts by encoded-key bytes (`canonical.js:157,172`)                                                                                                  | no consumer may depend on pre-encode insertion order                                                                                                                                             | seeded insertion shuffles in the differential fuzz                                                                                                      |\n   456\t| 6   | `structuredClone` vs canonical clone | reference clones only via `deepCloneCanonical` (`canonical.js:377-379`)                                                                                      | consensus state cloning is `deepCloneCanonical` **only**; ESLint `no-restricted-globals: structuredClone` in consensus packages                                                                  | state containing `-0`: canonical clone digests as `0`, `structuredClone` preserves `-0` — assert the lint rule exists and document the behavioural diff |\n   457\t| 7   | async WebCrypto vs sync noble        | `hash.js:55-58` is async-only                                                                                                                                | sync `@noble/hashes` on all digest paths and sync `@noble/curves/ed25519.js` on identity/signature paths (§2.6); WebCrypto only as a bulk Worker backend or for non-extractable seal-key custody | noble-vs-WebCrypto differential on framed inputs; type assertions that hashing and signing return `Uint8Array`, never `Promise`                         |\n   458\t| 8   | DataView endianness vs Buffer        | every DataView call passes explicit `false` (BE); no `Buffer` anywhere                                                                                       | only DataView with an explicit endian argument; `Buffer` banned by lint; registry pins BE                                                                                                        | golden typed-array vectors + lint gate                                                                                                                  |\n   459\t| 9   | locale-dependent sorts               | D1 above                                                                                                                                                     | `sortRule: \"codepoint\"` per registry field                                                                                                                                                       | D1's property test vs `Buffer.compare(utf8(a), utf8(b))`                                                                                                |\n   460\t| 10  | string length = UTF-16 units         | `assertString(value, …, maxLength)` counts UTF-16 units, not bytes (`protocol.js:17-20`)                                                                     | keep unit counting; registry documents the unit per limit                                                                                                                                        | astral-plane chars at the 1024/1025-unit boundary                                                                                                       |\n   461\t\n   462\t### 2.6 Crypto profile: sync `@noble/hashes`, and why §19's benchmark narrative is an artifact\n   463\t\n   464\tThe reference is async-poisoned end to end because `subtle.digest` is Promise-only (`hash.js:55-58`):\n   465\tadmission, vertex creation, Merkle append and QC building are all `async` **purely for hashing**, which also\n   466\tforces a top-level `await` into `ct-merkle.js:3`. The repo already ships sync SHA-256 everywhere\n   467\t(`packages/utils/src/hash/index.ts:1`; `@noble/hashes` is a production dependency of `object`, `utils`,\n   468\t`keychain`).\n   469\t\n   470\tAHE §19's slowest number — compact-accumulator Merkle at **739.8 ms median / 8192 leaves** — is dominated by\n   471\t~16k awaited WebCrypto round-trips over 64-byte inputs, not by hashing. In-browser runs here completed 2048\n   472\tappends in **50–87 ms** even under WebCrypto. So §19's \"pure-JS Merkle is the slowest path, consider WASM\"\n   473\tguidance is an artifact of the reference's crypto binding, not a property of the algorithm — and it must not\n   474\tbe used as a sizing input.\n   475\t\n   476\t_Decision:_ all protocol digests are **sync**, over `@noble/hashes/sha2`, behind one\n   477\t`hashDomain(domain, ...parts): Uint8Array` in `@ts-drp/canonical`, preserving the reference's exact framing\n   478\tbytes. Ed25519 identity and seal signatures are likewise sync over `@noble/curves/ed25519.js`; their\n   479\tprotocol functions return `Uint8Array`/`boolean` with no Promise surface. WebCrypto remains the custody\n   480\tbackend for non-extractable browser seal keys, not the protocol verification backend. `worker-host` MAY\n   481\teventually own the boundary for a bulk async backend for large snapshot/archive payloads, but that boundary\n   482\townership is **not** a Phase 2f closure requirement. Do not add a placeholder backend until a merged\n   483\tproduction UI-thread consumer exists and its exact representative payload measures release-matrix p95\n   484\tlatency of at least 50 ms. That trigger opens a new TDD contract which preserves the canonical sync owner\n   485\tand proves equality on shared vectors. Re-benchmark in-repo after the golden paths before considering WASM;\n   486\tprefer established prior art when it fits rather than inventing a new implementation.\n   487\t\n   488\t### 2.7 Wire format: the existing proto shape cannot carry v2\n   489\t\n   490\t`packages/types/src/proto/drp/v1/object.proto:7-19` carries operations as `google.protobuf.Value`, which\n   491\tcannot represent the canonical domain (bytes, `Map`, `Set`, typed arrays; every number becomes a double).\n   492\tA v2 vertex serialized through it loses byte fidelity, so hash verification either fails or — far worse —\n   493\t\"verifies\" over re-encoded bytes.\n   494\t\n   495\t_Rule:_ v2 proto messages carry `bytes canonical_preimage` + `bytes signature` (+ routing metadata only).\n   496\tReceivers decode via `@ts-drp/canonical` and **MUST verify the digest over the received bytes — never\n   497\tre-encode.**\n   498\t\n   499\t---\n   500\t\n   501\t## 3. The phase graph\n   502\t\n   503\tRound 1 landed AHE at Phase 3 (shadow snapshots) _before_ its then-proposed envelope at Phase 4. That\n   504\tordering cannot implement AHE directly: **a normative AHE snapshot payload commits to `objectId`, `epoch`,\n   505\t`sourceAnchor`, `schemaVersion` and `blueprintDigest` — none of which exist until the successor\n   506\tgenesis/anchor namespace does.**\n   507\tShadow-folding legacy state and calling it an AHE shadow validates the wrong pipeline. Likewise the history\n   508\troot and archive-index root are **mandatory fields of every cut and anchor**, so they cannot arrive in\n   509\tPhase 7 while Phase 6 already validates history continuity.\n   510\t\n   511\tThe corrected order is: **frozen-v2 baseline → oracles → determinism → substrate → v3 normative freeze →\n   512\tv3 namespace → shadow → seal → adopt/prune → archive**, with four parallel tracks.\n   513\t\n   514\t```mermaid\n   515\tflowchart TB\n   516\t    P-1[\"Phase −1<br/>Normative freeze (ATOMIC)<br/>registry · vectors · reference regen\"]\n   517\t    P-1P[\"Phase −1′<br/>Protocol-v3 normative freeze (ATOMIC)<br/>formal model · registry · refs · governance\"]\n   518\t    G0[\"Gate 0<br/>Oracles + divergence manifest<br/>+ CI topology\"]\n   519\t    P0[\"Phase 0<br/>Determinism core<br/>= port the pure modules\"]\n   520\t    P1[\"Phase 1<br/>Sync · auth · capacity · ops\"]\n   521\t    P2[\"Phase 2<br/>Durable substrate + Worker<br/>+ hard-kill driver + browser gate\"]\n   522\t    P3[\"Phase 3<br/>v3 namespace: genesis, anchor,<br/>admission, latched ACL, roots\"]\n   523\t    P4[\"Phase 4<br/>Shadow cuts + snapshots<br/>(no trust, no pruning)\"]\n   524\t    P5[\"Phase 5<br/>Seal: creator-certified →<br/>round-free → delegated → attested\"]\n   525\t    FM[\"Formal model (Quint+Apalache)<br/>STARTS AT EACH NORMATIVE FREEZE\"]\n   526\t    P6[\"Phase 6<br/>Verified adoption<br/>+ bounded pruning\"]\n   527\t    P7[\"Phase 7<br/>Archive / Discord profile\"]\n   528\t    TE[\"Track E — ephemeral plane\"]\n   529\t    TS[\"Track S — sharding\"]\n   530\t    TT[\"Track T — topology\"]\n   531\t    TP[\"Track P — production surface\"]\n   532\t    RG[\"Research gates\"]\n   533\t\n   534\t    P-1 --> G0 --> P0 --> P2\n   535\t    P0 --> P1\n   536\t    P0 --> P-1P\n   537\t    P-1P --> P3\n   538\t    P2 --> P3 --> P4 --> P5\n   539\t    P-1 -.-> FM\n   540\t    P-1P -.-> FM\n   541\t    FM ==hard gate==> P5\n   542\t    P5 --> P6 --> P7\n   543\t    P2 ==>|adoption CAS| P6\n   544\t    RG -.blocks.-> P5\n   545\t    RG -.blocks.-> P6\n   546\t    P1 --> TT\n   547\t    P1 --> TE --> TS\n   548\t    TP -.spans all.-> P7\n   549\t```\n   550\t\n   551\t**Why the formal model starts at Phase −1, not Phase 4.** The model validates the _design_ — spec plus the\n   552\tround-free `CutValue` amendment — and needs no implementation code. Round 1 started it at Phase 4, _after_\n   553\tthe atomic envelope freeze it is supposed to validate. If the model then discovers the pacemaker needs an\n   554\tadditional signed field, the only remedy is `protocolMajor = 3` — precisely the outcome the atomic freeze\n   555\texists to prevent. So the model starts with the registry, and **a mechanical variable-set sign-off gates the\n   556\tfreeze**: every signed envelope field must appear in the model's variable set.\n   557\t\n   558\t---\n   559\t\n   560\t## Phase −1 — Normative freeze (ATOMIC — nothing else starts)\n   561\t\n   562\t**Goal:** one interoperable protocol. Today there is not one: codec, hashes, cut identity, round change,\n   563\tprofile activation and adoption all disagree or are undefined. **No consensus byte is written until this\n   564\tlands.** Round 1 placed this as a Phase-4 \"precondition freeze\" in prose; a registry mentioned only in prose\n   565\tis not an executable slice, and freezing at Phase 4 forces either a double vector-freeze or months of\n   566\tunpinned porting.\n   567\t\n   568\t| Slice       | Change                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | Class        | Atomic?                              | RED test → GREEN                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n   569\t| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------ | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   570\t| **−1a**     | `packages/protocol-v2/registry/field-registry.json` — machine-readable: `domains{}`, `kinds{fields[{name,type,constraints,required,sortRule}]}`, `framing{}`, `endianness`, `quorum{}`, `actions[]`. Every hashed structure, its domain string, exact field order and encoding.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | consensus-v2 | **atomic**                           | `registry.test.ts`: preimage builders are constructed _from_ the registry field list; `expect(cutValuePreimage({...unknownField}))` throws; every registry entry is referenced by ≥1 vector (`expect(uncoveredFields).toEqual([])`)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n   571\t| **−1b**     | Codec + framing decisions (D1–D3, §2.4 and the table below)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | consensus-v2 | atomic with −1a                      | `canonical-adversarial.test.ts`: `ENC(Float32Array[-0]) === ENC(Float32Array[+0])` and both decode; changing the process locale cannot alter signer-set or QC bytes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n   572\t| **−1c**     | **Round-free `CutValue` / round-bearing `SealProposal` split.** This is a _preimage_ decision, not a Phase-5 implementation detail.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | consensus-v2 | atomic                               | `round-repropose.test.ts`: the same semantic value proposed in round `r` and `r+1` has an **identical `valueDigest`** and different `proposalHash`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n   573\t| **−1d**     | **Signature suite — two keys, one primitive, distinct suite identifiers.** (a) **Identity/vertex:** `ed25519-sha256-v1`, Ed25519 over the raw 32-byte registered digest. (b) **Seal voter:** `ed25519-seal-v1`, also Ed25519 over its raw registered digest. Distinct identifiers keep identity and seal independently rotatable; because the primitive is shared, the registry domains and exact `hashDomain` framing are load-bearing. Strict RFC 8032 verification (`zip215: false`) is consensus-critical. `p256-sha256-v1` is recognized but **reserved, not active**. Legacy-plane secp256k1 is untouched. | consensus-v2 | atomic                               | `signature-vectors.test.ts`: deterministic 64-byte Ed25519 vector; raw-digest rule; malformed length, ZIP-215-only and thrown-verifier inputs fail closed; identity-domain signatures do not verify as seal signatures or vice versa; reserved/unknown suites return exactly `false`. Scope labels are metadata: callers must recompute the registered digest from the canonical preimage under the declared registry domain.                                          |\n   574\t| **−1e(i)**  | **`cryptoSuiteId` permitted values enumerated and negotiated at genesis only** (never downgradeable — Phase 3b). Active: `ed25519-sha256-v1` (identity) and `ed25519-seal-v1` (seal). Reserved: `p256-sha256-v1`. There is no runtime fallback or downgrade.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | consensus-v2 | atomic with −1d                      | `crypto-suite.test.ts`: unenumerated and reserved suites reject with `UNSUPPORTED_PROFILE`; a peer lacking the genesis-named active suite rejects rather than substituting; epoch-anchor/profile enumerations must match and active/reserved sets must be disjoint                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n   575\t| **−1e(ii)** | Mint `registry-v5.json` from real registry-built preimages, including ordered `partsHex` for the actual domain-hash parts; preserve the original reference byte-for-byte; add `reference.lock.json`, semantic CODEOWNERS/workflow governance, and the base-governed silent-landing check                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | consensus-v2 | sliceable; **stacked on −1a…−1e(i)** | `golden-vectors.test.ts`: all registered kinds and active enum values covered; normalized-record `canonicalHex`, ordered `partsHex`, and digests agree in TS and the original reference; lock ↔ source ↔ vendored SHA-256 bijection; bootstrap registry bytes pinned; real temp-git CLI probes reject an invalid base, same-version registry drift, prior-vector drift, noncanonical vector path, bad regen provenance, policy weakening or protected-artifact drift |\n   576\t| **−1f**     | Regenerate the JS reference from the amended schema into the **separate** `ahe-reference-regen/` tree, by a **different author** than the TS port. Add `reference-regen.lock.json` with registry-v5, vector-v5 and original-lock provenance. Do not bump the registry. Then both references and the registry freeze forever.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | coordinated  | atomic                               | Both references reproduce every applicable vector byte-for-byte; regenerated tree/lock atomicity and provenance pass the base-governed checker; a post-regeneration registry bump fails                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n   577\t| **−1g**     | Spec amendments written into `docs/protocol/` with a versioned amendment log                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | —            | sliceable                            | Amendment log entry exists for every registry decision below                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n   578\t\n   579\t### The frozen decisions\n   580\t\n   581\t| #   | Divergence                                                                                                                                           | Decision                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n   582\t| --- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   583\t| 1   | Hash framing: spec §4.2 `H(domain ‖ U32BE(len(part)) ‖ part…)` vs `hash.js:60-68` `\"DRP\\0\" ‖ U32BE(\\|domain\\|) ‖ domain ‖ (U64BE(\\|part\\|) ‖ part)*` | **Reference wins.** Amend §4.2.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Length-prefixing the domain removes a domain/part boundary ambiguity the spec text has; the magic gives suite separation; U64BE removes a 4 GiB part cap; every existing digest and all bundle evidence already use it.                                                                                   |\n   584\t| 2   | Typed-array endianness: spec §4.1 little-endian vs `canonical.js` big-endian everywhere                                                              | **Big-endian.** Amend §4.1.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | Consistent with the framing integers; all evidence bytes are BE. (Note: the framing integers were never in conflict — both sides are BE. Round 1 mis-stated this.)                                                                                                                                        |\n   585\t| 3   | Codec identity: spec \"recommends CBOR\" vs the reference's proprietary tag codec                                                                      | **The tag codec.** Amend §4.1.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | Already implemented and browser-audited; zero new dependencies; CBOR would require rewriting the reference _and_ re-auditing a library's canonical-mode strictness for no protocol gain. Note this deletes \"indefinite lengths\" from the negative corpus — a CBOR-only concept.                           |\n   586\t| 4   | `-0`                                                                                                                                                 | **Reject on decode, normalize on encode — consistently, including `Float32Array`**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | Removes the D2 asymmetry.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n   587\t| 5   | Omitted preimage fields                                                                                                                              | cut += `archiveIndexRoot`, `availabilityPolicyDigest`; anchor += `archiveIndexRoot`, `blueprintDigest`; snapshot payload += `blueprintDigest`, `archiveIndexRoot`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | `archiveIndexRoot` is the Discord profile's entire audit chain; `availabilityPolicyDigest` is what makes §17's pruning gate objective; `blueprintDigest` is what turns a reducer-version mismatch from a silent fork into a loud rejection.                                                               |\n   588\t| 6   | `highestPrepareQC`                                                                                                                                   | **Does NOT enter the prepare/commit vote preimage.** It moves to the **round-change** message body, whose preimage the registry defines alongside phase `\"round-change\"`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | It is per-signer justification, not value binding. Binding it would make otherwise-identical votes digest-unequal and break QC aggregation.                                                                                                                                                               |\n   589\t| 7   | Quorum                                                                                                                                               | Registry pins `q(n) = ⌈2n/3⌉`, `f = ⌊(n−1)/3⌋`; **no caller-supplied `maxByzantine` on the consensus path**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | `seal.js:13-19`'s `⌊(n+f)/2⌋+1` is _identical_ to `⌈2n/3⌉` for `f = ⌊(n−1)/3⌋` across n=4..100,000 (verified by execution), but the reference lets a caller pass `maxByzantine` and break the equivalence.                                                                                                |\n   590\t| 8   | Sort rule                                                                                                                                            | All protocol sorts are UTF-8 byte order (`sortRule: \"codepoint\"`); `signerId` charset excludes control characters                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | D1. Also closes NUL key-smuggling in the vote key.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n   591\t| 9   | Conflict action set                                                                                                                                  | Freeze **five** actions (repo `ActionType` incl. `Drop`), not the reference's four (`linearize.js:157-162`); amend spec §7.2                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Existing blueprints use drop-both.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n   592\t| 10  | Trust profile and voter storage policy                                                                                                               | `profileDigest` + `cryptoSuiteId` are **explicit signed fields** in genesis and every anchor; profiles are exactly `creator-trusted-v1`, `delegated-trusted-v1` (n delegates, explicit quorum `k ≥ 2` — not BFT, honestly labelled \"k of these n must agree\") and `attested-bft-v1`. **Trust profile determines authority/quorum; storage class determines custody** (§Phase 5). `q = 1, n = 1` uses a recoverable seed-derived key plus network re-learn and MUST NOT fate-share. Every quorum-`≥ 2` voter preserves exact-slot anti-equivocation continuity: a detectable-loss durable-class voter uses durable CAS/outbox, incarnation binding and permanent refusal on mismatch; an external exact-slot witness is valid; a browser-local eviction-prone voter without that witness uses fate-shared non-extractable custody. Both delegated and attested sets admit both storage classes. Every set with eviction-prone voters includes a durable-class voter or declares stall acceptance; a stall never lowers quorum. | Separating authority semantics from storage/custody prevents the third trust-profile name from being confused with custody option 3, while keeping storage loss fail-closed.                                                                                                                              |\n   593\t| 11  | Naming                                                                                                                                               | `protocolMajor: 2`, domains `ts-drp/*/v2`, package `protocol-v2` are the only identifiers in code. \"AHE v4\" survives solely as the spec document title.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Round 1 correctly flagged the v2/v4 naming mess.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n   594\t| 12  | Signature suites                                                                                                                                     | Activate `ed25519-sha256-v1` for identity/vertex and `ed25519-seal-v1` for seal votes; reserve `p256-sha256-v1`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | One primitive with distinct suite identifiers preserves independent rotation. Keeping P-256 active would admit an ECDSA malleability/digest-identity fork for obsolete browser-local eviction-prone voters whose fate-shared custody option still lacks real-device co-eviction evidence (Appendix D.23). |\n   595\t\n   596\t### −1g amendment log — signature-suite reservation\n   597\t\n   598\tRegistry version 5 records `p256-sha256-v1` as reserved and activates `ed25519-seal-v1`. A future edit that\n   599\treactivates `p256-sha256-v1` **MUST, in that same edit**, pin low-S normalization and pin whether the\n   600\t32-byte registered digest is passed as the raw ECDSA message or prehashed again. Reactivation without both\n   601\trules is an incomplete consensus change and must fail review.\n   602\t\n   603\t### Exit gate (Phase −1)\n   604\t\n   605\tRegistry v5 merged; `registry-v5.json` immutable and pinned; original and separately regenerated references\n   606\tlocked under distinct manifests; regenerated provenance binds the registry, vectors and original lock;\n   607\tspec amendments merged with an amendment log. **Exit remains blocked until formal-model variable-set\n   608\tsign-off is recorded; Phase −1g documents the variables but does not produce that sign-off.** Mutation\n   609\tprobes demonstrate that same-version registry drift, any old-vector mutation, either reference/lock drift,\n   610\tchecker or policy weakening, and every post-regeneration registry change fail the always-run CI check.\n   611\tRepository branch protection must require the preserved `Require protocol-v2 registryVersion bump` status\n   612\tand CODEOWNERS review; those GitHub settings are an external release prerequisite and cannot be proven by\n   613\tan in-repo test.\n   614\t\n   615\t**Real-device mobile evidence is NOT a Phase −1 exit-gate item — it binds at the Pre-release release\n   616\tgate, after the whole feature set is green end-to-end.** An\n   617\tearlier draft of this section made archived real iOS/Android `Ed25519: non-extractable` runs a blocker on the\n   618\tfreeze. That was the wrong milestone, and it is corrected here. What that evidence gates is **non-extractable\n   619\tseal custody** for browser-local eviction-prone voters using custody option 3, in either a delegated or an\n   620\tattested signer set. It is not a property of the third trust profile and not part of the freeze. Three facts\n   621\tmake the deferral safe:\n   622\t\n   623\t- **Identity and vertex signing need no WebCrypto at all.** `signature.ts` uses synchronous\n   624\t  `@noble/curves` per §2.6. Every mobile engine — including pre-18.4 iOS and pre-137 Android WebViews —\n   625\t  participates fully in a v2 room as a non-voter regardless of what `crypto.subtle` supports.\n   626\t- **The interop gates that actually carry the freeze are green.** Browser↔browser convergence\n   627\t  (`examples/grid/playwright.modular.config.ts`, multi-browser cold start, rendezvous discovery, state sync,\n   628\t  relay-loss recovery) and node↔browser (`playwright.canvas-chat.config.ts`) both pass, and\n   629\t  `packages/node` is 202/202. Those are the paths a byte-level freeze can be wrong about; a mobile\n   630\t  key-custody capability is not one of them.\n   631\t- **Custody option 3 is independently gated anyway.** A browser-local eviction-prone voter without an\n   632\t  external exact-slot witness may enroll only with browser-specific evidence that key/log co-eviction is\n   633\t  atomic. A run that cannot produce that evidence cannot enable this storage mode in either delegated or\n   634\t  attested authority, so the capability measurement is subsumed by an existing gate.\n   635\t\n   636\tThe requirement therefore **moves, it is not dropped**, and it moves to the end of the plan rather than to\n   637\tPhase 5: real-device iOS and Android runs reporting `Ed25519: non-extractable`, with device, OS and\n   638\tengine/build recorded, are part of the **Pre-release tier's existing real-device sign-off** — the release\n   639\tgate that runs after the full feature set is complete end-to-end. Desktop Playwright mobile emulation does\n   640\tnot satisfy it at any milestone.\n   641\t\n   642\t**Why the end of the plan and not custody-option-3 enablement.** Mobile is a _deployment-target_ question, not a\n   643\tdesign input. Nothing upstream of the release gate needs the answer:\n   644\t\n   645\t- If a mobile engine supports non-extractable Ed25519, custody option 3 works there for either eligible\n   646\t  trust profile and nothing changes.\n   647\t- If it does not, that engine cannot enroll as a browser-local eviction-prone voter without an external\n   648\t  exact-slot witness. It may still use the durable class or witness mode where available and participates\n   649\t  fully as a non-voter because identity and vertex signing never touch `crypto.subtle`. Desktop and Node\n   650\t  seal voters are unaffected.\n   651\t\n   652\tSo the measurement can only ever change _which devices may hold seal keys_ — never the frozen bytes, the\n   653\tregistry, or any interface built between here and there. Deferring it costs no rework: by the time the\n   654\tgolden paths are green, confirming mobile is either trivial or the suite decision gets revisited\n   655\tdeliberately, which is exactly what the reserved identifier exists to permit.\n   656\t\n   657\t**What the deferral costs, stated plainly:** if a release-matrix mobile engine turns out to lack\n   658\tnon-extractable Ed25519, custody option 3 is unavailable there — the same failure mode −1d cites against\n   659\tsecp256k1 — and we discover it at the release gate rather than now. **Reserving rather than deleting\n   660\t`p256-sha256-v1` is what makes that recoverable**: the identifier is still claimed, and reactivation is a\n   661\tdocumented, rule-bound edit (−1g) rather than a new protocol negotiation. That is an acceptable trade\n   662\tbecause the\n   663\talternative was keeping `p256-sha256-v1` active, which was a _measured_ consensus-byte defect (D.23) rather\n   664\tthan a hypothetical one. Note also that this desktop matrix was wrong once for ~16 months, so the standing\n   665\ttest's fail-on-any-change discipline (2j) is what protects the assumption in the meantime.\n   666\t\n   667\t### Phase −1a–c runtime hardening boundary\n   668\t\n   669\tThe registry runtime strips undeclared keys from typed nested structures, matching the pinned reference's\n   670\tnormalization rule. Untyped canonical values remain open by design. The checked-in registry is deeply\n   671\timmutable at runtime, every registered kind has a unique domain, and semantic constraints used by the\n   672\tcurrent slices must affect validation rather than merely being recognized.\n   673\t\n   674\tVertex admission owns the registered `ts-drp/vertex/v2` digest verifier. Dependency resolution is an\n   675\tordered envelope contract: every resolved parent is hash-, object-, protocol-, epoch-, anchor- and\n   676\tlogical-time-checked before antichain and grounding checks. A resolver result is never advisory.\n   677\t\n   678\tQC byte construction rejects empty or header-mixed vote sets. Signer membership, signature verification,\n   679\tand the `SUBQUORUM` decision remain Phase 5 responsibilities because they require the active signer set and\n   680\tthe signature suites frozen in −1d. Genesis-only continuity enforcement for `cryptoSuiteId` remains −1e;\n   681\tthe −1a runtime validates the declaration but does not simulate a prior anchor. These are explicit API\n   682\tboundaries, not permissive success paths.\n   683\t\n   684\tProtocol-v2 mutation runs use whole-suite mode (`coverageAnalysis: \"off\"`, Vitest related-test selection\n   685\tdisabled). Module-initializer mutants that the runner cannot activate are reported separately from the\n   686\tmutation score rather than counted as behavioral survivors.\n   687\t\n   688\t---\n   689\t\n   690\t## Gate 0 — Oracles, divergence manifest, CI topology\n   691\t\n   692\t**Why first:** under direct adoption the oracle is not merely a safety net for a future rewrite — it is the\n   693\tport's conformance harness from day one, and the reference already runs green here, so the second engine\n   694\texists immediately rather than at Phase 3.\n   695\t\n   696\t**The identity-vs-divergence correction.** Round 1's G-b asserts _byte-identical_ digests between the legacy\n   697\tengine and the candidate. That is the **wrong relation**: 0b (replacement adoption), 0c (classifier) and 0d\n   698\t(Kahn order) are _deliberate_ behaviour changes. After the first one merges the oracle is permanently red,\n   699\tand a permanently-red gate gets deleted or muscled green — the exact theater failure the plan warns about.\n   700\t\n   701\t| Slice   | Change                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | RED test → GREEN                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n   702\t| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   703\t| **G-a** | History recorder: capture real vertex DAGs from the existing proptest/e2e suites into replayable fixtures. On load, **recompute every vertex hash and assert equality** so fixture rot is loud.                                                                                                                                                                                | Fixtures committed; integrity check fails on a mutated fixture                                                                                                                                       |\n   704\t| **G-b** | **Divergence-manifest oracle** (replaces byte-identity). Fixtures replay through legacy + candidate; the oracle emits the _pair_ plus the order diff. Gate asserts: every differing pair matches an entry in `fixtures/divergence-manifest.json` (`{fixtureId, legacyDigest, candidateDigest, reason, specSection}`), **and** every manifest entry is exercised by ≥1 fixture. | `differential-replay.test.ts`: unknown pair → fail; **stale entry → fail** (`expect(unexercisedEntries).toEqual([])`). CODEOWNERS-gated; adding an entry requires the spec section that justifies it |\n   705\t| **G-c** | Golden counterexample against the **real** `dfsTopologicalSortIterative` (`hashgraph/index.ts:274-333`), not a model of it: deps `0←root, 1←0, 2←1, 3←1, 4←{0,2}` → full-tail `(2,4,3)` vs contracted `(2,3,4)`. Note `{0,2}` is non-antichain, so under the v2 rule vertex 4 is rejected at admission — assert divergence on the **tail set**, not a spurious mismatch.       | Runnable RED on HEAD today                                                                                                                                                                           |\n   706\t| **G-d** | **Mutation probes.** Every oracle/differential gate ships a seeded defect it must catch. Extends `packages/object/tests/proptest/mutation-check.test.ts`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Per gate: `expect(runGate(withSeededDefect)).rejects.toThrow()`                                                                                                                                      |\n   707\t| **G-e** | **CI topology.** `conformance.yml` (PR-blocking), `formal-model.yml`, `nightly.yml`, `weekly.yml`, `shadow-soak` (long-running deployment, not a CI job), `playwright.protocol-v2.config.ts`. Every gate in this plan annotated `blocks-merge` or `reports-only`.                                                                                                              | A canary PR injecting a digest mismatch fails the blocking check                                                                                                                                     |\n   708\t| **G-f** | **Coverage contract.** Per-package thresholds (`protocol-v2` ≥95/90, `seal` ≥95/90, `storage-browser` ≥90/85, `compaction` ≥90/85, others ≥80/70) **plus a zero-coverage allowlist**: any file below 5% must be listed with a justification; CI diffs the allowlist against reality and fails on unlisted zeros.                                                               | Probe test with a temp zero-coverage file must fail the gate                                                                                                                                         |\n"}],"tool_call_id":"tool_P7sr4NEgIUEol6MkEMLfpGs9"}
+{"role":"tool","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system>300 lines read from file starting from line 87400. Total lines in file: 88315.</system>"},{"type":"text","text":" 87400\t`1afb2c4feb8cbdf305213930c7d60ce8466fa2f7d37ad0d46e907dca5628a5da`.\n 87401\tThe 18-entry self-excluding manifest at\n 87402\t`.logs/d108e6-green/ARTIFACT-SHA256SUMS` validates completely and has SHA-256\n 87403\t`4f214f64b90de303e44a2fb4ea06602c0f334ae40b1df396877d87d3b5dce31d`.\n 87404\tProtected untracked paths and all 26 stashes remain untouched; no retained\n 87405\tcampaign ran. The single final plan-to-RED-to-GREEN review is the only\n 87406\tremaining D.108e6 and Phase-6a exit gate.\n 87407\t\n 87408\tThe single final review inspected plan closure `20d2ba09`, RED `9443c922`,\n 87409\tGREEN `4e4548b0` and evidence record `6e51852d`. Grok 4.6/high session\n 87410\t`01a058ca-5aef-7113-972a-70135e399242` was canceled during its initial\n 87411\tinspection and the exact session was resumed rather than replaced. Its first\n 87412\tresume completed inspection but exhausted turns while emitting invalid\n 87413\tnon-schema prose, so it remained `NO_VERDICT`; the same session resumed only\n 87414\tto emit its completed verdict and returned schema-valid `APPROVED` with no\n 87415\tfindings. The terminal resumed-result SHA-256 is\n 87416\t`69cc693791796caebe3032a772001196a88e05190e29ede7906c2e8f5511693f`.\n 87417\tCodex `gpt-5.6-sol` high session\n 87418\t`01a058ca-58d0-7300-89c5-f88f90539523` returned `APPROVED` with no findings;\n 87419\tresult SHA-256 is\n 87420\t`226d4f0d27bab4e9a5d843201e70c3e2947dcba569dbb1899478595efaf725a9`.\n 87421\tOpus 5/xhigh session `5f524924-203c-4b6c-8771-56bab7b8fd30` returned\n 87422\t`APPROVED` with no P0/P1 and four P2 observations; raw-result SHA-256 is\n 87423\t`f2775d11650a67255c7fad0cff54271a760a60ca3c6cb74a79f985c64bdbe920`.\n 87424\t\n 87425\tThe P2 set is recorded as nonblocking with no new slice. The exact pre-lint\n 87426\tRED preimage was not separately artifacted, but rule identity, erased\n 87427\tinterfaces, unchanged imports and byte-identical executed assertion excerpts\n 87428\tunder a uniform two-line offset independently corroborate the recorded\n 87429\ttype-only correction. A partial recursive-`mkdir` failure before root creation\n 87430\tcould leave one empty parent, but the next run fails closed on that artifact.\n 87431\tNon-`ENOENT` `lstat` errors may throw synchronously rather than reject, but\n 87432\tPlaywright awaits setup and these exotic/hostile-local states are outside the\n 87433\ttrusted serialized slice. No named control reaches `ENOTEMPTY` on a\n 87434\tsetup-created parent and exact command strings are not separate artifacts;\n 87435\t`rmdir` is intrinsically non-destructive for nonempty parents, parallel\n 87436\tauthority is explicitly declined, and result/status/path/hash evidence is\n 87437\tcomplete. None is a product defect, pass-fabrication path under the stated\n 87438\tmodel, or reason for more review ceremony.\n 87439\t\n 87440\tThe final P0/P1 union is empty. D.108e6 closes all remaining D.108e1 exit\n 87441\tledger observations without changing product behavior, and D.108e1 through\n 87442\tD.108e6 are complete. Phase 6a exits on this signed record; no retained\n 87443\tcampaign, threshold change, dependency change or product/API widening is\n 87444\tauthorized or required.\n 87445\t\n 87446\t### D.109 — Phase-6b bounded-pruning slice freeze\n 87447\t\n 87448\tPhase 6b begins after signed Phase-6a closure\n 87449\t`67833426279112e79aebaa16846ef4f7af6cfaf9`; that closure and all earlier\n 87450\timmutable evidence remain accepted and are not reopened. The executable slice\n 87451\tspecification is `specs/phase-6b-bounded-pruning/README.md` with D.109a through\n 87452\tD.109f under its `slices/` directory. The specification is subordinate to this\n 87453\tplan’s frozen wire, digest, QC, adoption, availability, rollback, outbox,\n 87454\tbrowser-dispatch and legacy-finality contracts.\n 87455\t\n 87456\tThe source audit found three distinct physical domains rather than one atomic\n 87457\tdatabase: AHE generations/blobs in the primary storage adapter, issued/outbox\n 87458\trows in the dedicated issuance adapter, and graph/state/checkpoint/cache data\n 87459\tin runtime memory. Phase 6b therefore makes no false cross-database atomicity\n 87460\tclaim. One package-internal cleanup planner owns eligibility. Each physical\n 87461\towner transactionally rechecks its own exact head/revision, row, closure,\n 87462\treference and lifecycle facts immediately before deletion. Stages are\n 87463\tidempotent and return immutable receipts. Runtime reclamation requires the\n 87464\tmatching durable receipts. A crash may retain extra data; no partial stage may\n 87465\tdelete data whose owner-local precondition changed.\n 87466\t\n 87467\tThe ordered implementation is:\n 87468\t\n 87469\t1. **D.109a eligibility:** deterministic immutable planning and closed refusal\n 87470\t   taxonomy only; no I/O, deletion, schema, scheduling or public export.\n 87471\t2. **D.109b issuance retention:** a separate identity-gated maintenance\n 87472\t   capability performs store-local atomic classification and deletion of only\n 87473\t   a complete valid paired `published` closed-epoch prefix, advancing one\n 87474\t   durable per-scope pruning watermark in the same transaction. Pending,\n 87475\t   malformed, one-sided, foreign, gapped, non-monotone or changed rows cause\n 87476\t   zero writes. At/below-watermark consumed absence is pruned rather than\n 87477\t   corruption; late acknowledgement returns exact non-poisoning\n 87478\t   `ISSUANCE_RECORD_PRUNED` and cannot claim unprovable exact-digest success.\n 87479\t3. **D.109c AHE reclamation:** a separate identity-gated maintenance capability\n 87480\t   performs store-local exact-head/closure/reference recheck, deletion of\n 87481\t   selected superseded generations/promotions, and GC of only newly\n 87482\t   unreferenced blobs while the active generation plus exactly the two complete\n 87483\t   rollback ancestors reached by following `baseExpectedHead` twice remain\n 87484\t   complete. Two other countable superseded generations cannot substitute.\n 87485\t   The same transaction preserves parent connectivity by normalizing only the\n 87486\t   oldest retained ancestor's exact expected `baseExpectedHead` to the existing\n 87487\t   no-head form before deleting the complete older connected prefix; any other\n 87488\t   surviving edge into that prefix aborts. Global blobs require a transactional\n 87489\t   decode-scan of every remaining cross-object closure and promotion row; no\n 87490\t   nonexistent reverse index is assumed.\n 87491\t4. **D.109d runtime reclamation:** after matching durable receipts, reclaim the\n 87492\t   enumerated graph, state, checkpoint, pending and sync structures while\n 87493\t   preserving root, active tail, canonical state, truthful known-hash\n 87494\t   inventory, and all legacy finality until Phase 6d.\n 87495\t5. **D.109e browser scheduling:** extract and reuse the exact Phase-5c advisory\n 87496\t   primary-dispatch runner, migrate the vote dispatcher to that sole owner, and\n 87497\t   prove lock-mode/takeover equivalence. A lease never grants deletion\n 87498\t   authority.\n 87499\t6. **D.109f exit:** ≥100-epoch archival-versus-compacted differential,\n 87500\t   raw-dependency audit, crash/reopen and browser takeover matrices, and exact\n 87501\t   census of every Phase-6b structure before Phase 6c memory work.\n 87502\t\n 87503\tRandom generation IDs are never treated as chronological order. The verified\n 87504\tlineage names the active generation and its two immediate `baseExpectedHead`\n 87505\tancestors; both ancestor rows and every blob in their closures must be present.\n 87506\tBecause current adoption validates that every present parent has a row, a D.109c\n 87507\ttransaction may delete older generation rows only while 