+{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"think","think":"D.109b used 8 paths; D.109c uses 9 (adds `tests/assets/phase-6b-ahe-reclamation-worker.ts` for the worker-termination crash matrix). That's justified by the Chromium worker termination requirement. Consistent.\n\nNow check the browser worker/IDB crash feasibility: existing precedent like phase-6a-creator-adoption-commit.pw.ts or phase-5c-seal-vote-death.pw.ts with worker termination. The plan asserts \"Chromium worker termination/IDB abort at each named mutation/commit edge can prove old XOR complete-new without changing production configuration, dependencies, schemas, or runtime behavior.\" Existing process-death and worker-termination harnesses exist (phase-2e6-real-process-death, phase-2h-d-process-death, phase-5c-seal-vote-death). The D.109c worker asset is a new fixture, allowed as tests/fixtures/config. Feasible.\n\nOne question: browser IDB transaction abort mid-transaction — to inject abort at specific mutation edges, the browser maintenance needs test-only abort hooks. Existing kill-point injection subpath precedent (\"kill-point injection subpath\" in the plan §2.1 description of storage-browser). So feasible.\n\nNow requirement 3 details: \"The request revalidates exact head/revision, active adopted generation, the two immediate superseded rollback ancestors, exact oldest-retained floor, expected former parent, no-head replacement, and the complete older prefix.\" — plan step 1–3 covers all. Empty-prefix and lost-receipt replay semantics covered (idempotence section). Decidable: yes.\n\nRequirement 4: transaction verifies retained and selected closure/promotion/blob facts (step 4), globally decodes every generation and promotion across all objects before candidate blob deletion (step 5, 9), preserves shared references (step 9), normalizes only retained floor (step 7), exact delete/update counts (steps 7-9), post-state verification (step 10). Staged/Discarded partial promotion sets (step 5). ✓.\n\nRequirement 5: error partition — INVALID_ARGUMENT (malformed caller input, never poisons), RETRY_REQUIRED (changed valid observations), CORRUPT (malformed durable facts, impossible partial replay → latch poison), STORE_CLOSED/STORE_POISONED precedence, SUBSTRATE_FAILURE no false success. Asynchronous rejection. \"No error exposes persisted bytes.\" ✓ matches plan lines 124-141.\n\nRequirement 6: memory critical section (TransitionOwner is synchronous; maintenance can run within... hmm, memory facade methods return Promise.resolve(owner.x()) — the maintenance method would need to run synchronously inside. The plan says \"No await or externally supplied callback may split the memory critical section\" — feasible since TransitionOwner is sync and JS is single-threaded; an async function that never awaits between mutations is atomic. ✓). SQLite BEGIN IMMEDIATE exists in precedent. Strict IDB transaction over four AHE stores — existing `allAuthorityStores()` used for recovery with strict durability; a transaction over the four AHE stores is a subset. ✓. SIGKILL crash observer infra exists; the maintenance path needs its own crash edges — plan acknowledges \"package-owned test-only crash observer\" inside Node transaction. Test-only fault edges added at GREEN batch 2 — the crash observer parameter on `SqliteAheDurableStore` constructor already exists, extended for new operations. Hmm — does extending the SqliteCrashCheckpoint type to include new operations constitute a production-code change? Yes, but it's GREEN production change (allowed — GREEN adds production code; RED forbids production changes). The plan freezes RED as tests-only; GREEN adds the maintenance implementation including test-only fault edges. ✓.\n\nRequirement 7: nine-path RED tests/fixtures/config only ✓. Three readiness tokens ✓. Semantic/mutant/crash roster: covers identity denial, input capture, positive control, idempotence/replay/concurrency, stale lineage variants, corruption poisons, shared-blob/global scan, count-mismatch rollbacks, Node SIGKILL six edges, Chromium abort edges, retained D.109a matrix. Causal mutant sufficiency: the roster includes \"wrong-but-countable rollback pair\" (inherited from D.109a P1 correction), surviving branch, extra row, dangling post-state. Is there a missing causal mutant? Possible gaps:\n\n- \"two-live-handle serialization\" is listed.\n- Blob deletion without global scan would be caught by \"shared blobs ... across an unrelated object\" cases.\n- Deleting floor without normalization → caught by \"subsequent genuine creator-adoption commit\" case (the dangling-parent oracle retained).\n- Count mismatch injection → rollback completeness.\n\nOne potential gap: the requirement says \"Identify any infeasible fixture assumption or missing causal mutant.\" Consider: a mutant that deletes blobs of the retained rollback closures... covered by \"preserving active/rollback bytes\". A mutant that normalizes the wrong floor (\"wrong floor\") covered. A mutant that skips the post-state verification? That's an internal-implementation mutant; RED can't easily seed arbitrary internal mutants — but the roster is about behavior cases.\n\nPotential infeasible fixture assumption: the browser worker crash at \"after-blob-delete\" and \"before-commit\" — IDB auto-commit semantics: if you terminate the worker mid-transaction, the transaction aborts. To place a kill exactly \"before-commit\", the worker code must signal the harness at the edge and then be terminated. Feasible with a test-only hook that posts a message then waits. This is a standard pattern here (phase-2e6 etc.). OK.\n\nAnother check: \"reclaimClosedEpoch(input: unknown): Promise<AheReclamationReceipt>\" — the memory registry: \"Memory registers its capability through a package-private identity registry\" in @ts-drp/storage. Fine.\n\nNow, subtle correctness checks on the algorithm itself:\n\nStep 7: \"rewrite only the second rollback/floor record's baseExpectedHead from the exact expected parent to the existing no-head form. ... re-encode canonical storage v1 bytes.\" — Wait: does rewriting `baseExpectedHead` change the generation's identity? Generation identity is `generationId`; the record includes closureDigest etc. Is `baseExpectedHead` part of any digest? closureDigest covers closure only. Head fingerprint covers head. So rewriting baseExpectedHead of the floor doesn't change digests. But: `Authorization` in closure-verifier binds `encodeGenerationRecordV1(generation)` — full record including baseExpectedHead. That's per-session, not persisted. OK.\n\nBut does the parent-presence predicate in adoption require `baseExpectedHead` of floor to match anything? validLineage requires each generation's present parent to exist in the surviving set; no-head is fine. And note in creator-adoption.ts `recoverClosure` adopted-branch: requires proposed.baseExpectedHead present matching currentHead etc. — the floor is older than that pair, unaffected.\n\nOne more: after floor normalization, the floor's `baseExpectedHead` becomes `{kind:\"none\"}`. On later reclamation runs (epoch e+1 closes, active moves forward), the new floor's prefix walk terminates at the no-head floor. The D.109a planner walk: `while (cursor.kind === \"present\")` — terminates at no-head; then `cursor.objectId !== activeHead.objectId` check. Works with a mid-chain no-head floor. ✓ Repeated reclamation across epochs is feasible.\n\nRevision chain check in lineagePlan: `cursor.revision !== expectedRevision - 1` — after floor normalization, the floor's record no longer has a parent but its own identity unchanged; the floor's *children* reference floor with revision-1 — unchanged. ✓.\n\nStep 2-3: \"Require ... exact head/closure-digest/revision links\" — revalidates.\n\nNow check requirement 9: no schema change (SQLite tables unchanged; IDB stores unchanged; floor rewrite reuses existing record encoding — the generation record v1 codec already encodes baseExpectedHead as ExpectedHead including none-variant — verify codec supports kind none. `encodeGenerationRecordV1` in storage/src/codecs.ts. beginGeneration accepts no-head base (genesis), so codec supports it. ✓).\n\nNo reverse index needed: step 5/9 do full scans. ✓.\n\nNo mandatory facade change: 12-key facade unchanged, maintenance via subpath+WeakMap. ✓ (D.109b precedent accepted).\n\nNow let me look for potential P1-level issues more aggressively.\n\n**Issue candidate 1: Memory backend's poison semantics for reclamation.** The plan says CORRUPT latches \"the ordinary store latches poison\". Memory TransitionOwner has `latchIntegrityFailure` — private. GREEN adds a TransitionOwner mutation which can latch. Fine.\n\n**Issue candidate 2: IDB strict transaction durability** — Chromium supports `durability: \"strict\"`. Existing code already requires it (`StrictDurabilityCapabilityError` if not). ✓.\n\n**Issue candidate 3: The plan says \"Browser issues every cursor/request from the one live transaction and performs no unrelated asynchronous work that could auto-commit it.\"** — This is an implementation discipline requirement; fine for a plan.\n\n**Issue candidate 4: global decode-scan of every generation across all objects inside one IDB transaction** — bounded? Unbounded scan inside a strict transaction could be long-lived, but correctness-wise fine; IDB transactions stay alive as long as requests are issued. Plan-aware.\n\n**Issue candidate 5: The receipt `deletedBlobDigests` — exposes digests of deleted blobs.** Requirement: \"No receipt contains blob bytes...\" — digests are fine per plan (D.109b's exception was about not claiming exact-digest success for deleted rows; here the receipt lists deleted blob digests — they're caller-known from the plan closures... wait, actually the D.109c input does NOT include closures. The input has only generation IDs. So the deleted blob digests are discovered during the transaction. Is exposing deleted blob digests a problem? The plan says receipts must not contain blob bytes; digests are content-addressed identifiers the caller could have known from the generation records... but the caller only has the D.109a plan, which includes... does the D.109a plan carry closures? No — ClosedEpochCleanupPlan carries only identities, not closures. So the receipt's `deletedBlobDigests` reveals digests the caller may not have had. Is that a defect? Requirement 5 says \"do not expose persisted bytes\" — digests are not bytes of content. The D.109b P1-era concern was about *success claims for deleted digests*, not exposure. This seems acceptable; not a demonstrated blocker.\n\n**Issue candidate 6: empty-prefix semantics vs D.109a.** D.109a GREEN note: when floor is already no-head, deletion prefix empty, \"later stage is an idempotent no-op rather than a fabricated rewrite\". D.109c input validation: \"an empty deletion list is valid only when the expected floor parent is already that same no-head value\". And idempotence: \"An initially empty prefix with a no-head floor is a no-write success.\" Consistent. But wait — for the empty-prefix case, `expectedBaseExpectedHead` would be `{kind:\"none\", objectId}` and replacement the same. But D.109a lineagePlan: when floor (second rollback) has baseExpectedHead.kind === \"none\", the while loop doesn't execute, prefix empty, and expectedBaseExpectedHead = no-head. ✓.\n\nHmm, wait — one subtlety: for empty prefix, is `expectedBaseExpectedHead` equal to the replacement? Input validation says empty list valid only when expected floor parent \"is already that same no-head value\". ✓.\n\n**Issue candidate 7: Lost-receipt replay detection.** \"accepted only when every requested prefix row and promotion is absent, the exact floor is already no-head, head/active/two rollback graph still matches, retained closure evidence complete.\" But how does the transaction distinguish \"replay of my own committed run\" from \"someone else deleted the prefix\"? It can't — but the deletion is only valid if the exact same preconditions hold, and the receipt only claims reclaimed (proven absent) with no fabricated writes. If someone else's identical request deleted the prefix, replay acceptance is safe because the resulting state is exactly what this request would have produced (same floor, same prefix). This is sound.\n\nBut consider: a *different* head (new adoption happened after the prefix was deleted). Then head/active/rollback graph won't match → retry. ✓.\n\nConsider: prefix rows absent AND floor already no-head, but this is *initial* state — i.e., D.109a planned a nonempty prefix but the rows were never there (fabricated input)? Can the input be fabricated by a caller with a made-up prefix? The caller could pass any deleteGenerationIds. Replay acceptance requires floor already no-head and prefix absent and retained evidence complete. If a malicious caller fabricates a request where the prefix rows never existed and floor is already no-head — the replay path would \"accept\" and report reclaimedGenerationIds as that prefix, with zero writes. Is that harmful? The receipt claims those generations are \"proven absent at commit\" — true, they are absent. No deletion occurred, no harm. The receipt cannot be used to mint authority for anything else (\"no future-deletion grant\"). This is benign. ✓.\n\nBut wait — the mixed case: \"absent selected row with the old floor still present is corruption\". If the floor's parent is present-form pointing to a deleted row, that's a dangling parent — corruption of durable state, poison. Right: that state breaks the parent-presence invariant, so poisoning is the safe response. ✓.\n\n**Issue candidate 8: Two rollback requirement when active generation is at revision 2 with only one rollback.** D.109a refuses (insufficient). D.109c input requires both rollback rows present. ✓.\n\n**Issue candidate 9: The plan claims RED adds \"tests/fixtures/config only\" with \"package manifests ... byte-identical\".** But the browser RED needs `playwr